-- title:
--   Godement–Jacquet functional equation for cuspidal Whittaker coefficients
-- statement:
--   Fix a nonzero prime $p$ of $\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q}$, write $F=\mathbb Q_p$ for the completion, $q=\operatorname{absNorm}(p)$, $|\cdot|$ for the module `modulus` of $F$ and $\psi$ for the standard local additive character `psiLocal`. Given a character $\theta_0:F^\times\to\mathbb C^\times$, a nonzero ideal $N$, and a function $w_2:\mathrm{GL}_2(F)\to\mathbb C$ satisfying $w_2\big(\begin{smallmatrix}1&x\\0&1\end{smallmatrix}\big)g)=\psi(x)w_2(g)$, right invariance under the level subgroup [`AdelicDock.localLevelOne`](def/AdelicDock_LocalEmbedding.html#L178) of $N$ at $p$, $w_2\neq 0$, the irreducibility condition that every nonzero element of the span $V$ of the right translates of $w_2$ generates $w_2$ in turn, the admissibility condition that for every open subgroup $U$ the $U$-right-invariant vectors of $V$ lie in the span of a finite set, and $w_2(zI\cdot g)=\theta_0(z)w_2(g)$; given $w_J$ with matrix $\big(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{smallmatrix}\big)$, a locally constant $\chi:F^\times\to\mathbb C^\times$, $E_0\in\mathbb C$ and $e_0\in\mathbb Z$. Assume (hfe) that every $w\in V$ admits $P,P_d\in\mathbb C[X]$, $m,m_d\in\mathbb Z$, $\sigma_0,\sigma_1\in\mathbb R$ with: $y\mapsto w(\mathrm{diag}(y,1))\chi(y)|y|^{s-1/2}$ integrable for $\operatorname{Re}s>\sigma_0$ against the multiplicative measure induced on $F^\times$ by the self-dual additive Haar measure, with integral $q^{ms}P(q^{-s})$; $y\mapsto w(\mathrm{diag}(y,1)w_J)\chi(y)^{-1}\theta_0(y)^{-1}|y|^{1/2-s}$ integrable for $\operatorname{Re}s<\sigma_1$ with integral $q^{m_ds}P_d(q^{-s})$; and $q^{m_ds}P_d(q^{-s})=E_0q^{e_0s}\,q^{ms}P(q^{-s})$ for all $s$. Assume (hcusp) that each $v\in V$ has $v(\mathrm{diag}(y,1))=0$ for $|y|$ small. Then for every Haar measure $\mu_2$ on $\mathrm{GL}_2(F)$, every $w\in V$, every locally constant compactly supported $\Phi$ on $M_2(F)$, all $P,P_d,Q,Q_d\in\mathbb C[X]$ with $Q,Q_d\neq0$, all $m,m_d\in\mathbb Z$ and $\sigma_2,\sigma_3\in\mathbb R$: if $g\mapsto w(g)\Phi(g)\chi(\det g)|\det g|^{s+1/2}$ is $\mu_2$-integrable for $\operatorname{Re}s>\sigma_2$ and $\mathrm{godementZeta2}(w,\Phi,\chi;s+\tfrac12)\,Q(q^{-s})=q^{ms}P(q^{-s})$ there, and $g\mapsto w({}^t g^{-1})\,\widehat\Phi(g)\chi^{-1}(\det g)|\det g|^{s+3/2}$ (with $\widehat\Phi=\mathrm{matFourier22}$ of $\Phi$ against $\psi$) is integrable for $\operatorname{Re}s>\sigma_3$ with $\mathrm{godementZeta2}(w\circ{}^t(\cdot)^{-1},\widehat\Phi,\chi^{-1};s+\tfrac32)\,Q_d(q^{-s})=q^{m_ds}P_d(q^{-s})$, then for all $s\in\mathbb C$ one has $q^{m_ds}P_d(q^{-s})\,Q(q^{s})=E_0q^{-e_0s}\,q^{-ms}P(q^{s})\,Q_d(q^{-s})$.
--
--   This is the local Godement–Jacquet functional equation on $\mathrm{GL}_2$ for a Whittaker function taken as matrix coefficient, in the cuspidal case where all Kirillov functions of the representation vanish near $0$, stated in cleared-denominator form: the rational identity $Z^\vee(s)=E_0q^{-e_0s}Z(-s)$ is expressed as an identity between the numerator polynomials and the clearing factors $Q,Q_d$, valid for all $s$ without convergence restrictions. It feeds the combined statement [`LanglandsTunnell.RankinSelberg.forall_godementZeta2_whittaker_clearedFE_of_forall_torusZeta_fe`](thm.html#LanglandsTunnell.RankinSelberg.forall_godementZeta2_whittaker_clearedFE_of_forall_torusZeta_fe), which assembles the cuspidal and non-cuspidal cases.
